#define CULTURE_WAYS_LEN (451)

static const s_CultureWays[CULTURE_COUNT][CULTURE_WAYS_LEN char] = {
	!"Swedish traders carry furs, iron and copper along the Baltic to Novgorod and Gotland. Farmers behind them grow rye and barley and keep cattle. Disputes go to the local ting, judged by neighbours and kin. Their late conversion means old feast days stand beside the new ones. They dress in wool and sheepskin. A traveller finds beer and fish at their table. Steady work earns a freeman real respect.",
	!"A Norwegian farmstead is held by odal right, land kept in the same family for generations, out along the fjords and up into the high valleys. Farmers keep goats and grow barley through short summers. Fishermen dry their catch into stockfish to sell at Bergen. The king's law now reaches every fjord, though a strong local chief still earns real loyalty. A Norwegian judges you by your deeds and says so plainly, without flattery.",
	!"Rank shows in every greeting a Dane gives, from a neighbour's easy nod to the formal words saved for a lord. Their king's fleet has carried the cross across the Baltic to Estonia and still guards the sea lanes home. Cattle and grain from the fertile lowlands feed a table of rye bread, pork and herring. Having taken the faith earlier than their neighbours, they keep church feast days with real ceremony.",
	!"German townsfolk build their standing on a craft. People greet each other by that standing first, master, knight or burgher alike, ahead of any given name. Guilds set the rules of trade in every town and defend their charters before lords and bishops. Knights answer to an emperor or a duke under an oath they take seriously. Rye bread, pork, cabbage and beer fill their tables. Wool marked by rank tells everyone a person's place at a glance.",
	!"A stranger notices old English custom sitting next to newer Norman manners. Equals just clasp hands or bow, while a lord is addressed as sir or my lord. Disputes go before a jury as often as a lord's own court, and a freeman's sworn word counts for a great deal. Nobles still speak French at court and in law. At home most people speak English. They live on wool, ale, bread and mutton. Fair play matters more to them than showy manners.",
	!"Frisians raise cattle on terp mounds, earthen hills built up out of marshland they reclaimed from the sea. No count or duke rules over them, only their own elected leaders and old customary law they wrote themselves. They speak their own tongue and trade by sea along the North Sea coast, answering no lord's call beyond defending Frisia itself. Plainspoken and stubborn about their freedoms, they guard their independence jealously.",
	!"Dutch townsfolk earn their keep in the cloth trade, under guild rules that matter more day to day than any lord's word. Much of their land came from the sea, won back by dikes and drainage that whole towns build together. A burgher values a fair bargain, honest weight and a debt paid promptly over any noble title. They eat bread, herring and cheese and drink beer. Plain good wool covers them day to day, with finer clothes kept for guild feasts.",
	!"French manners set the standard that every other court in Europe copies, from a knight's courtesy to the polished speech at noble courts. Knights dress by strict rank and hold to codes of honour, owing service to a lady or lord as much as to their own name. They eat bread, wine and pottage at table, rich or poor alike. Even a peasant keeps faith with a home village's patron saint, a loyalty that comes before any oath sworn to a lord.",
	!"Normans trace their blood to Norse raiders who became French-speaking knights. They run their land with real precision. Since 1204 their old duchy answers to the French crown, which made families with land on both sides of the Channel pick England or France. A Norman keeps a castle at the heart of the land he holds, from England to Sicily. They swear loyalty on holy relics before God and expect the vow kept to the letter.",
	!"An Italian city measures a person by wealth and guild standing as much as by noble blood. A merchant or banker can hold real power in the council chamber. Communes elect their own officials and guard their civic freedom against both emperor and pope. They dress richly whenever they can afford it and eat bread, olive oil, wine and pasta at a merchant's table. Family honour and loyalty to one's own quarter of the city matter as much as money does.",
	!"Occitans are southerners with a language of their own, the tongue of oc. Troubadours are loved here. A man who can sing or turn a clever phrase is respected as much as a good fighter. Northern knights came south to crush the Cathar heretics, and the Inquisition that followed has made people careful when they talk about faith. They grow grapes and olives and live on bread, wine and stewed vegetables. Strangers get a warm, easy welcome.",
	!"Romans live among ancient ruins where pilgrims and clergy from every land pass through on their way to Saint Peter's tomb. Noble families feud for influence over the papal throne, while guildsmen and innkeepers make their living off the pilgrims. Latin stays the tongue of church and law, though people speak their own Roman dialect at home. They eat bread and stewed vegetables, dress modestly, and greet a stranger with pious hospitality.",
	!"Basques hold to fueros, charters of their own that no distant king can override. They judge a stranger mainly by the respect he shows those rights. Mountain shepherds and coastal fishermen alike prize their independence fiercely and stay close to family first. Devout Catholics, they still keep customs from the mountains older than the church here. They dress in wool and leather and warm to strangers once trust is earned.",
	!"Castile is a frontier, shaped by generations of holy war against Moorish neighbours. Even common folk here stay austere and devout because of it. They dress plainly and eat bread, mutton, chickpeas and wine at a modest table. Sheep and cattle herding under the great Mesta drives much of their wealth, and minor nobles value honour, lineage and service to the king over money itself.",
	!"Catalans build their fortunes on Mediterranean trade. Barcelona's merchants govern their own affairs under charters and a maritime law respected across the sea. They speak their own language and keep their count-king's power balanced against their assembly's consent. A fair contract counts for as much here as noble blood does. They eat bread, olive oil, wine and seafood and greet a stranger with brisk, businesslike courtesy.",
	!"The Portuguese are proud not to be Castilian, and have been since their kingdom was founded. Many of them live off the Atlantic as fishermen and sailors. Their military orders and border nobles are crusaders at heart, raised on generations of war against the Moors. On the coast people eat bread and sardines with their wine and dress simply. They're polite but reserved with strangers. Family loyalty and patience count for a lot here.",
	!"Armenian merchants and craftsmen trade hard in the markets, but any guest still eats lavash bread, cheese and wine before business starts. They greet with a handclasp or a kiss on both cheeks and speak formally to elders and strangers. Their own Apostolic Church marks feast days with incense, carved cross stones and long fasts. Clan ties come before any deal. Children grow up with deep respect for priests and parents.",
	!"Byzantine Greeks keep icons, saints' days and long fasts at the centre of daily life, breaking them with olives, bread, fish and watered wine. Formal manners matter more than birth. Even a stranger of rank gets a bow and the correct title. Loyalty to emperor and bishop earns lasting respect, as does educated speech. Guests are welcomed warmly, though outsiders are watched for manners before they're trusted.",
	!"Alans hold to steppe guest right, feeding a stranger meat and fermented milk as soon as he sits by their fire. Raised in the saddle from childhood, they hire out as heavy cavalry to Byzantium and Georgia. Greek missionaries baptized them Orthodox, though old customs hold on. Clan and horse lord loyalty outweighs any distant king, and quarrels get settled by the sword.",
	!"Among Georgians, an insult to a guest can start a blood feud that outlasts the men who began it. Family and clan loyalty comes before almost everything else. A guest is treated as a gift from God, seated at a long table loaded with bread, cheese, walnuts and wine, with toasts to God and ancestors expected before anyone eats. Devout Orthodox Christians, they value chivalry and verse over wealth.",
	!"Assyrian scribes chant their liturgy in Syriac. Their Church of the East prizes literacy so highly that many serve as physicians and translators on the roads into Persia and beyond. Merchants and craftsmen of modest dress, they greet formally and defer to priests and elders, keeping strict fasts. Bread, lentils and dried fruit fill most meals, and marriage stays inside the church.",
	!"A traveller at an Irish door gets food and drink even from a poor household, since hospitality is a matter of honour under brehon law and turning someone away brings real shame. Poets and harpers rank near lords, feared for satire as much as praised for verse. Cattle measure a family's wealth, and having children raised by allied families cements old bonds. A person's clan comes before any crown.",
	!"Highland clan chiefs command a loyalty that traces back through generations of kin and outweighs any order from a distant king. A stranger asking for shelter has to be fed and housed, or the household is shamed. Wrapped in plaid wool and fed on oatmeal, barley bannock and beef, Highlanders answer slights with the sword. Devout in the old church, they keep wakes for the dead and value blunt speech.",
	!"Sheep and cattle are the chief wealth of the Welsh, though a man's worth is reckoned by his lineage and poetry just as much as by his herds. A bard's praise or satire can make or ruin a name overnight. The old law of hospitality guarantees any guest food, a bed and protection, and turning one away shames the household. Mead and ale wash down oatbread and meat. Tempers flare fast here, and blunt talk is common.",
	!"Bretons welcome a traveller on their coast with bread, cider and a seat by the fire. They farm and fish this windswept shore, keep their own tongue, and hold deep devotion to local saints, visiting shrines and holy wells for blessing and healing. Fisherfolk share the catch with neighbours as a matter of course. Kin ties and parish loyalty matter more than any distant lord, and plain wool dress marks an honest household.",
	!"Finns clear forest for rye and till small fields between spells of hunting, fishing and trapping fur. Disputes get settled calmly, often after everyone has cooled off in the sauna. Old songs and charms carry law, lore and healing, even as priests spread the new faith through the north. Guests get rye bread, fish and buttermilk without fuss. Plainspoken and slow to trust outsiders, Finns value quiet self-reliance.",
	!"Sami noaidi shamans drum and fall into trance to read fortune and heal the sick, though a few now hear Christian priests too. Families hunt wild reindeer and elk, fish and trap fur, keeping only a few tame reindeer to pull sledges and lure the wild herds close. They shift camp with the seasons, living in hide tents, and trade furs south to Norwegian and Russian merchants. Joik songs name people and places. Meals run to fish, meat and berries.",
	!"Estonian folk songs, passed voice to voice, carry the history and belief of kin villages that live behind earthen hillforts. Crusading knights and priests demanding tribute get met with spear and axe from a people who defend their land fiercely, though a peaceful guest gets food and a roof the moment he arrives. Farmers tend rye, barley and cattle, gathering at sacred groves to honour the old gods of forest and field.",
	!"Komi households of the northern forests live by hunting, fishing and trapping fur to trade with Novgorod merchants, filling out their meals with rye bread and berries. Most honour forest and water spirits and turn to a shaman when sickness or bad luck strikes. Kin and village elders settle disputes, and a skilled trapper is valued as highly as a rich farmer further south.",
	!"After a hunt, Khanty families hold a great bear feast, singing and performing for days to honour the animal's spirit before a shaman guides the rite. Families live along river forests by fishing, hunting and modest reindeer herding, moving between winter log houses and summer camps. Wooden idols carved for river, forest and sky spirits stand watch over each home. Furs pay tribute to distant princes, and hunting skill wins real standing.",
	!"Nenets clans hunt the wild reindeer herds across the frozen tundra, following their migration between coast and forest, and keep a small tame herd for milk, hide and sledge travel. Fishing and seal hunting fill out the larder. Families live in hide chum tents that shift with the seasons. Shamans call on Num, the sky god, and guard against the spirit Nga with offerings. Novgorod traders collect fur tribute from them. Clan bonds run strict.",
	!"A guest in a Mordvin village on the Volga gets fed generously, often on wild honey and mead as much as bread and meat. Villagers farm rye and keep bees, prizing a good hive the way others prize a good harvest. Women wear striking dress heavy with bronze jewellery marking their rank. Most honour Ineshkepaz and the nature spirits at sacred trees, despite pressure from neighbours to convert. Elders hold authority here.",
	!"Lettigallians farm and raise cattle around river hillfort strongholds, trading amber and furs with Russian principalities to the east. That trade has brought early contact with the Orthodox faith among their nobles. Chieftains lead their own loose bands of kin, with no single crown over all of them, and hospitality to travellers marks a household well. Feasts include mead, rye bread and river fish, and ancestor grave mounds get defended fiercely.",
	!"Lithuanians honour Perkunas with thunder, tend sacred groves and keep an eternal flame burning, resisting conversion longer than their neighbours. Warrior nobles ride to war and to the hunt, prizing horses, amber and mead above most things. A chief's hall must feed any guest well or lose face before its own people. Lithuanians keep expanding east into Rus forests, and their princes marry into Christian courts without giving up the old gods.",
	!"Old Prussian clans answer to no single king. Free men gather in open councils to settle disputes and choose war leaders when needed, and a guest at the fire always gets fed first. Their high priest, the Krive, tends a sacred grove where every tribe sends offerings and settles grievances under oath. Amber gathered from the sea is their famous wealth, traded away for grain and metal all along the coast.",
	!"A stranger asking a Turkish household for bread and shelter gets fed at once, since hospitality reflects on a household's honour. Settlers here are moving from tent camps into towns, keeping herds of sheep and horses while taking up trade. Devout Muslims guided by imams, they pray five times a day. Elders and holy men earn real respect, and evenings pass over flatbread, yogurt and mutton stew.",
	!"Cuman clans raise carved stone statues over their dead, honouring ancestors and marking battles won in life. They worship Tengri the sky and Eje the earth mother through shamans, though some later take up Christian or Muslim faith. Roaming the steppe in felt yurts mounted on wagons under strict guest right, they herd horses and sheep across open country. Many Cuman women end up marrying into Rus princely houses.",
	!"Volga Bulgars pray toward Mecca in mosques built since their ruler sent to Baghdad for teachers, and Islam now shapes daily life across the river towns. They still keep herds of cattle and horses alongside a thriving river trade. Their city on the Volga draws merchants trading furs, honey, wax and slaves between the northern forests and the Muslim south. Elders and imams are held in high regard.",
	!"Karluk Turks graze horses and sheep across the steppe near the old trade roads, wintering close to oasis towns under their protection. Long since Muslim, they pray in mosques built generations back. Turkic titles and steppe manners stay much the same. Once vassals of the Qara Khitai, their lords now answer to Mongol khans but keep their own towns and law. Felt tents, mutton and kumis remain common as leaders settle into town life.",
	!"Yenisei Kirghiz bury their dead under stone cairns marking the herds and deeds a man won in life, with shamans reading omens and guiding the rites. They herd horses and yaks through river valleys and northern forests, prizing skill with bow and horse above nearly everything. Chiefs lead the clans, but tribute and warriors now go to the Mongol khans who conquered these forests. Felt tents and fermented mare's milk get them through hard winters.",
	!"Uyghurs work irrigated gardens around their oasis towns, growing melons and grain to go with meat and milk from the herds. They move between winter palaces and cooler summer camps, blending old steppe horsemanship with city comforts. Many follow the teachings of Mani or the Buddha. Their scribes keep careful accounts for merchant caravans, using a script that other peoples later borrow, and a good education counts for a lot here.",
	!"A guest inside a Mongol ger gets fermented mare's milk and dried meat right away, since turning anyone away shames the household. Mongols raise children in the saddle and judge a person by horsemanship, archery and loyalty to clan and khan. Their round felt gers move with the herds across open grassland. Under Tengri the eternal sky, any faith a guest follows is tolerated, and women manage household and herds with a real say.",
	!"Khitans once ruled a great realm in the east, but fled west after its fall and built a new one in the mountains of Central Asia. There they rule Muslim farmers and townsfolk under their own law, keeping their customs apart from those they rule. Horses, cattle and falconry from childhood mark a Khitan household. Women hold real power beside the men. Old sky spirits and the Buddha are honoured at feasts of meat and mare's milk.",
	!"Jurchen shamans call on ancestor and sky spirits at clan shrines and guide the healing rites that follow sickness or a bad omen. Along the wild rivers of the north, families hunt, fish and herd horses, prizing strength and archery above soft living. They train falcons for the hunt as a mark of standing. Having conquered farmland to their south, Jurchen lords enforce their own dress and customs there by law, ruling apart from their new subjects.",
	!"Bedouin have a saying that a guest earns three days of food and shelter before he says why he's come. Sheikhs lead through counsel instead of command. Tribal honour settles most feuds and marriages. Sunni Islam shapes their prayers and diet. Dates, milk and roasted meat get shared freely at the fire. Women keep the herds while men raise camels, guide caravans and prize a sharp word as much as a blade.",
	!"Berber markets along the Atlas and the Maghreb trade salt, wool and leather between oasis towns. Tamazight is what they speak at home, even as Arabic spreads through prayer and trade. They're mostly Muslim, though tribal councils let elders settle disputes by agreement. Women often wear tattooed marks and silver jewellery, men a hooded wool cloak called a burnous, and both work mountain terraces and herd goats.",
	!"Levantine hosts in Damascus, Aleppo and the port of Latakia offer sweet syrups and pastries before trade talk begins. Muslims, Christians and Jews trade side by side in the same streets, each keeping its own feasts and fasts. Fine linen and silk sashes mark a household's standing. Artisans work whole quarters given to one guild. A stranger asking for shelter rarely leaves empty-handed.",
	!"Egyptian fellahin farm land that belongs to distant lords, paying rent in grain that follows the Nile's flood calendar. Most follow Sunni Islam, though a Coptic minority keeps its own churches and fasts in the same villages. Bread, beans and lentils fill most tables, cotton and linen the common dress. Cairo's scholars and merchants give the city its fame for learning. Pilgrims bound for Mecca are welcomed along the way.",
	!"Andalusians of Granada greet with elaborate courtesy, sliding between Arabic and the odd Castilian word. Jewish neighbours still share the crowded quarters of the city. Poetry and music are prized in any household that can afford a musician. The bathhouse is where the day's gossip gets traded. Their Muslim kingdom pays tribute in silver and silk to keep the peace with Castile.",
	!"Orthodox Christianity sets the rhythm of a Russian year, its fasts, feast days and icons kept in the corner of every home. Guests are met with bread and salt, since turning away a stranger under one's roof is thought to insult God himself. Princes command loyalty, and families work the land together. Rye bread, cabbage soup and honeyed mead are common fare, belted tunics and heavy fur in winter the usual dress.",
	!"Volhynians farm rich borderland between Poland and the Rus lands. Their loyalties are split between Orthodox and Catholic neighbours, and between the prince and his boyars, who often hold more power than he does. They're polite to visitors but wary of newcomers, since armies often cross their fields. Life follows the Orthodox feast days. They eat hearty grain dishes and wear embroidered linen shirts. Traders and soldiers fill their walled towns.",
	!"Pomeranian port towns along the Oder ship amber, salt and herring across the Baltic, small harbours whose trade outweighs their size. Christianity arrived here later than in neighbouring lands. Wooden shrines to old Slavic gods once stood where a church now stands, and saints' days still share the calendar with older customs. Greetings are plain here. Any household with room by the hearth takes in a traveller.",
	!"Catholic saints' days and processions mark the Bohemian year. Silver under the hills has new mining towns rising fast. German settlers come from across the border to work them. Plain-spoken courtesy counts for a lot. A firm handshake and visible respect for guests, elders and one's own lord all matter here. Hearty meals of pork, dumplings and cabbage go down with the beer the region is famous for.",
	!"Poles greet travellers with an old line, a guest in the house is God in the house, and welcome them with bread, salt and mead the moment they arrive. Devout Catholics, they fill the calendar with Mass and saints' days feasting. Nobles ride and hunt from childhood, and a good seat in the saddle earns more respect than a clever tongue. Rich stews, sausages and rye bread fill the common table.",
	!"Croatian coastal towns trade wine and oil under Venetian influence, busy with fishing boats and merchant hulls, while inland Slavonia stays a country of grain fields and cattle. Catholic in faith, a few parishes hold a rare privilege, the right to say Mass in Old Slavonic from Glagolitic books instead of Latin. Greetings run warm here. Loyalty splits between the local lord and the Hungarian crown.",
	!"Orthodox faith shapes daily life among Serbians, its monasteries covered floor to ceiling in fresco and its feast days marked without fail. Kin and guests alike are met with a kiss on both cheeks and a warm word. Extended families share one household under an elder's word. An oath once given matters more than any amount of wealth. Every gathering brings out the gusle, the fiddle that carries the old epic songs.",
	!"Vlachs eat cheese and yogurt made from their own flocks and wrap themselves in thick felt cloaks against the mountain cold. Their Latin-rooted tongue sets them apart from every Slavic neighbour around them. Flocks of sheep and goats move between high pasture and lowland valley with the turning seasons. Whole clans travel under one chosen leader. Orthodox in faith, they keep patron feast days timed to the herding calendar.",
	!"Bulgarians point to Cyril and Methodius whenever a stranger asks about their letters, since theirs was the first Slavic tongue written and prayed in its own script. Orthodox Christian, they mark saints' days with village feasting and song. Grain farming and vineyards keep most families fed, while boyar nobles hold the surrounding land. Proto-Bulgar blood mixes with Slavic custom in their wedding rites.",
	!"Hungarians pour wine and pile on roasted meat the moment a guest sits down. Descended from steppe horsemen, they still prize riding, archery and hunting above nearly every other skill. Their Magyar tongue shares nothing with any neighbour's language. Catholic in faith, they mark saints' days and harvest feasts with music and dancing. Nobles favour coats trimmed in fur, and herders judge a stranger by his manners at the table.",
	!"Persians rarely say a plain hello, favouring instead a proverb, a formal title, or a small bow with one hand to the heart. Poetry and philosophy get read aloud at almost any gathering, alongside the Islam most Persians practice. Sherbet, rice and grilled meats fill a hospitable table, fine silk robes and tall caps marking a person's standing. Scribes are valued for administrative skill.",
	!"A Kurdish chieftain's word settles most disputes long before any distant king's law would reach these valleys. Any guest under a chief's roof gets fed, sheltered and defended like family. Most Kurds follow Sunni Islam, and their older mountain customs still sit comfortably alongside the mosque. Herding sheep and goats between summer and winter pasture shapes the whole year, alongside weaving the woolen rugs the region is known for.",
	!"Afghans of the mountain passes live by an old and unwritten code, lavish hospitality for any guest, an answer in kind for any wrong and shelter even for an enemy who begs it. Tribal councils of elders settle most disputes in these valleys. Sunni Muslim in faith, most herd goats or guide caravans through the passes toward India. To an Afghan, a fast horse and an unbroken word of honour beat any pile of riches.",
	!"Baloch guides lead caravans across a harsh, dry homeland between Persia and India, one of the few steady trades this land offers. Camel and goat herds move at a chief's word, which usually settles any dispute before it grows serious. A host guards a guest's safety even at his own cost, and old grudges still get answered in kind. They speak their own Balochi tongue and wear long, embroidered tunics.",
	!"A handshake or a touch on the shoulder opens most Ethiopian greetings, with questions about family and health coming first. They belong to the Ethiopian Orthodox Church, keep its long fasts, and bow to priests wearing white cotton to services. Injera bread topped with spiced wat stew is the daily meal, eaten from one shared plate. Elders, monks and the negus command deep respect, and a traveller is fed as soon as he arrives.",
	!"Clan and elders run Somali life, settling disputes under xeer, their own law sealed by oath. Nearly all are Muslim, greeting with peace be upon you and prizing a sharp turn of phrase in proverb and poetry. Herders and traders, they eat rice, camel milk and meat, and wear long cotton wraps against the heat. A guest gets food and shelter right away, with questions saved for after.",
	!"Painted Coptic churches anchor Nubian life in Makuria and Alwa, their walls covered in saints, their services in Greek and the Nubian tongue. Farmers work the Nile's flooded banks, raising sorghum, dates and cattle, wrapped in white linen against the heat. Strangers meet calm courtesy and a shared meal. Bishops and the king have final say in most matters, and land and rank follow the mother's line in a family.",
	!"Griots, hereditary bards, recite genealogies and epics such as Sundiata's to keep Mande honour and history alive. Greetings run long, with blessings and news of family exchanged first. Most follow Islam alongside older customs of honouring ancestors. Millet and rice in sauce make up the daily meal, and kola nuts mark a welcome. Dyed cotton robes are the everyday dress, and elders and guests both receive deep, careful respect.",
	!"Friday sundown brings the Sabbath into every Ashkenazi home, lit with candles and set with wine and challah bread. Boys start studying the Talmud at thirteen in the yeshiva attached to the synagogue, the heart of a self-run kehilla. Yiddish is the everyday language, prayer stays in Hebrew, and shalom aleichem is the common greeting. Kosher rules shape the kitchen, dress stays modest, and rabbis have the final say when neighbours disagree.",
	!"Sephardi Jews live in the aljama, a self-governing quarter of an Iberian town with its own rabbinic court. Prayer is in Hebrew. Their poetry and manners owe a lot to their Muslim and Christian neighbours. They wash their hands before entering the synagogue and study scripture and philosophy. At home most speak Arabic or the local language. The family gathers for the Sabbath meal of bread and wine. Travellers and scholars are always welcome.",
	!"Rice paddies and jute fields fill the delta Bengalis call home, worked alongside daily fishing on its rivers. Most are devout Hindus, many drawn to Vaishnava worship of Krishna and Vishnu at the village temple, where an elder's word settles most questions. Betel leaf follows every meal, and rice with fish and lentil curry is the everyday plate. Cotton suits the delta's damp heat, and folded hands make the usual greeting.",
	!"The temple of Jagannath at Puri owns farmland, feeds pilgrims by the thousand and keeps whole villages at work as priests, cooks and dancers. Once a year its great chariots roll through the streets, and lord and commoner stand equal before the god. Rice country feeds the region, served with vegetable and fish curry. Cotton is the everyday dress, and any pilgrim on the road gets food and shelter for the asking.",
	!"Muga silk from Assamese looms travels far beyond these hills, prized alongside the rice and betel nut grown in the Brahmaputra valley. Hindu worship blends easily with older nature and ancestor rites. Rank shows in dress and in how a house gets built. Visitors are met warmly and offered betel nut and rice beer. Elders and chiefs of the village earn lasting respect, and clan ties and river trade hold the community together.",
	!"An elaborate bow or a formal word of welcome opens most Hindustani greetings, with real respect for elders and rulers. People speak Hindavi, a blend of Sanskrit, Persian and Arabic. They move with equal ease between the Hindu temple and the Sufi shrine. A guest is fed and made comfortable, with questions saved for later. Farmers of wheat and rice, they dress in cotton tunics and prize poetry and calm, polished speech.",
	!"Trade built Gujarat's fortunes. Its merchants, many of them Jain or Hindu, once financed kings and whole caravans from this western coast, and a travelling merchant always finds a welcome. Respect for all life keeps many strictly vegetarian, and funding a temple or rest-house counts as a real act of merit. Fine cotton and silk are the everyday dress. Honest dealing matters more than bravado here.",
	!"Clan elders settle most disputes in Panjabi villages, meeting together in council. Wheat farming and cattle herding fill the days, and wrestling and plain hard work earn real respect. Many honour Sufi saints at local shrines alongside their temple gods. Guests get fed lavishly on wheat bread, lentils and buttermilk. Simple cotton is the common dress, and plain speech wins out over flattery.",
	!"A guest at a Rajput door is sacred, fed and defended the moment he arrives. These warrior clans live by a strict code of honour, ranking loyalty to their king and courage in battle above life itself. A given word can't be broken. Any insult to family or clan is usually answered by the sword. Turbans mark clan and rank, swords are worn openly, and women guard the household's honour and its estates.",
	!"Sufi shrines along the Indus draw music, pilgrims and devotion from every faith. Sindhis are famed for that mystic bent, favouring kafi songs and poetry over dry argument. Farmers and fishermen work the river's flooded fields for a living. A stranger is met with warmth and a blessing. Block-printed ajrak cloth in red and indigo is the everyday dress, and elders and holy men are heeded closely.",
	!"Millet and jowar grow on the hard, rocky soil Marathas farm across the Deccan plateau. Plain toughness beats any ornament here, and hill forts get watched closely. Many follow the Warkari path, walking to Pandharpur regardless of caste and singing the hymns of their poet-saints to Vithoba. Folded hands greet a visitor, and simple grain and lentil meals suit the working life this land demands.",
	!"A Sinhala king proves he's the rightful ruler by keeping the Buddha's tooth relic safe. No reign stands without the blessing of the sangha, the Theravada monks. Wars in the north have pushed many families south, to farmland fed by monsoon rain instead of the old reservoirs. Rice goes to the monks as alms each morning. Palms pressed together greet a visitor, and plain wrapped cloth is the everyday dress.",
	!"Namaste, said with folded hands, opens most greetings among Nepalis in the hill kingdoms. Hardy farmers of the terraced slopes, they herd goats and grow barley and rice in narrow valleys, offering travellers a warm hearth along the way. Hindu worship blends with older nature rites, honouring Shiva and the mountain spirits alike. Village priests and elders get consulted before any major decision. Wool and rough cotton keep out the cold.",
	!"Great stone temples anchor Tamil village life, employing dancers, priests and craftsmen and holding land and wealth for the community. Most worship Shiva or Vishnu with real devotion, and caste rank is observed carefully in daily dealings. Folded hands greet a visitor. Rice from the river delta, eaten off a banana leaf, is the daily staple. Elders and temple authority get obeyed, and guests and pilgrims are always fed first.",
	!"Merit counts for more than birth among the Telugu, since any capable man can earn the warrior title of nayaka and rise in his king's service. Building temples and irrigation tanks is a lasting act of honour. Hindu worship here stays tolerant of Jain neighbours. They keep their own Telugu tongue alive alongside the Sanskrit of court and temple. Folded hands greet a visitor, and a stranger gets judged by conduct alone.",
	!"Guilds of sculptors raised the star-shaped stone temples that still stand across Kannada country. Faiths mix here without conflict, Shaiva, Vaishnava and Jain worship side by side. Official decisions get recorded in both Kannada and Sanskrit, and merchants, monks and travellers move through the land safely. Farmers and traders alike eat rice and millet with vegetable curry, and folded hands are the common greeting.",
	!"A slight bow with hands pressed together, sometimes a white scarf offered in welcome, is how Bodpa greet a guest. Up on the high plateau they raise yak and sheep and grow barley through short summers, ground into tsampa and eaten with butter tea. Most follow Buddhism, often alongside older Bon rites, honouring their lamas and hanging prayer flags for merit. Thick wool and sheepskin keep out the cold, and hardship gets borne without complaint.",
	!"Tanguts farm irrigated fields and herd livestock along the steppe's edge. They work as middlemen in trade between China and Central Asia. Most are devout Buddhists who build monasteries and translate scripture into their own script, yet still call on shamans before any war or big decision. Wool and felt are the everyday dress, mutton and grain the everyday meal.",
	!"Respect for parents, ancestors and elders comes first in a Han household, with Confucian duty shaping seating and speech at any gathering by strict rank. A bow, or a clasp of one's own hands, is the usual greeting. Calligraphy, poetry and composed manners are valued highly, and serving tea to a guest marks a warm welcome. Rice or wheat noodles fill the daily bowl, and silk marks the well-off."
};

stock Culture_GetWays(culture,dest[],size = sizeof(dest))
{
	if (!Culture_IsValid(culture))
	{
		dest[0] = EOS;
		return;
	}

	strunpack(dest,s_CultureWays[culture],size);
}
